#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "b10aaa3e-2ba4-51af-be3b-056d72692885" :question/math-academy-id "e-10889" :question/problem "![](images/23/23e7d18cc240e7cf3937d11fd6484c3cce1027c237c85430f69f17bf25f0f487.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given above. Find $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac32.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in [1,2].$ Notice that $x=\\dfrac32$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/90/9067715198a510cc1399bb257352babb1ac093db1c80c611c2236c10b43607bc.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-2}{2-1}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9811f16e-75aa-5982-b97b-cc59b33fb7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10890" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6ae60cb8606fb75aa49251ad894ce6a56173d6d5310aba0018e6c6933d1b93fe.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(-1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x) + g'(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(-1) =f'(-1) + g'(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,1)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c6ff6240abc7b56f058547e29ef250514fd19723057a65089ef0fa6da0ec044.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{1-(-1)}{1-(-2)} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(-1)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{-1-2}{0-(-2)} = -\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g'(-1)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & ={f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "1fb2491b-f442-57e3-990f-65bf1270d847" :question/math-academy-id "e-10891" :question/problem "![](images/69/69dd5f35d420746ebecc4fc7b01f09f0be01ccbc9b02cc3987533be5c0ec58ad.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(0)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot g'(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(0)=f'(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot g'(0).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(0)=1$ and $g(0)=-1.$ \n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(-1,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, $f'(0)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(-0.5,1)$ equals $2.$ So, $g'(0)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & ={f}^{′}(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot {g}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5e929c25-68b1-5a6b-92a0-d12b90a112e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10892" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cd56facb5599cc8f2ffd8de8ee152cfec47844fc96d3daddb42e59afb0c8a57.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find $h'(2)$ where $h(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = \\dfrac{f'(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot g'(x)}{(g(x))^2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(2) = \\dfrac{f'(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot g'(2)}{(g(2))^2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $g(2)=0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, $f'(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-0.5.$ So, $g'(2)=-0.5.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{{(g(2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (0.5)-(1)\\cdot (-0.5)}{(0.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6a9e11-3036-53f8-ac15-d01d693e07e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478626} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6e7b3d-d036-5298-bcb3-3f7d1a5c3f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478674} {:answer/id #uuid "6628780b-eaea-5735-a579-9fedebcc91a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478709} {:answer/id #uuid "f483a7cc-6a66-5c31-a0d7-9c5ee6c3d1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478943} {:answer/id #uuid "f48ae967-9cec-52e7-bae0-020c6e476122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48ae967-9cec-52e7-bae0-020c6e476122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478944} :answer-field/id #uuid "99868b60-186f-522c-b404-7e8e762c2c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b26b207d-effc-596c-ae01-16ed4d0fdbd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104774" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b63554ba2724862e30569687e53e2068eda0024052d49a163b6aa2097c2ba659.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[2,4].$ Notice that $x=3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/35/357d426d58f6dd77a618fd30ad3735ef6a77763f77387be3181bd5149f973488.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-5-(-1)}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09155d3d-b192-5bae-a605-135f81cb4f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238148} {:answer/id #uuid "2a27fa9b-f9eb-571f-9ad7-adb78ba23899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238426} {:answer/id #uuid "3e212e53-c68a-501b-9fcb-1d4edff07617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238597} {:answer/id #uuid "9b25ef98-e399-524d-a61e-1e8de15956dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239408} {:answer/id #uuid "bffb9468-e4e2-5fde-8305-8df58aeb2faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e212e53-c68a-501b-9fcb-1d4edff07617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238597} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a281e5c-a134-5ef8-b7b0-5dd10c9df750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66a00b85-1c8b-5d71-a8f5-a30ecfdeb00b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104782" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a11d5b84bdf66b6406f9465ba6a8bc8ecdf364bea2dcd6902034990a74ef0a9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-3).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(-3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-5,-2].$ Notice that $x=-3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/59/59159512fa1b51477f171584a3717e2bf2774e8c940b59525fe478e1bcf6aece.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{-2-(-5)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238348} {:answer/id #uuid "6a281104-3172-5207-85ac-d3523e9f06ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238976} {:answer/id #uuid "7523980a-3dc9-556f-a900-bd8d74b02fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239067} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5b7892-34f3-551d-a7a4-706e26b36bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240143} {:answer/id #uuid "fc55fbbe-3f5a-500d-a655-ae7d80f5fb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238348} :answer-field/id #uuid "16fa28a0-1995-57c0-b2e6-fa809fab21ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6c1e529-59cb-55e7-b759-671e209b110e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104785" :question/problem "![](images/df/df4fed951351dfc1203ba6257f404a5efadbec37b9f11098af1fc36a7793b0c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=g(x)-f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)-{f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/81/814ce0fdce1428b3bd619caa5e8d09a828d2f2c52fd0b20b7f520562319fb848.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{4-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e41277-3773-5e50-b48b-3e07ec725687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238104} {:answer/id #uuid "1a00f49c-5c8e-5e22-91ce-65f418c55c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238271} {:answer/id #uuid "24144e35-1ecf-550b-9f0d-0e56bd4dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9a5995-a974-5e53-80dd-de5ec2c79e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239121} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ad6ad5-505a-576a-9124-401f591be2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24144e35-1ecf-550b-9f0d-0e56bd4dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238373} :answer-field/id #uuid "88a678ca-db36-56d8-badf-56a0bf3dbb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d460086c-0f96-558c-85a2-2debf9c57ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104787" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e02f873d2d583767e0b29e7ad57121e23e5c73716ce058ef6bed09b644b37bce.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f64bb3d174950f3c4ac26d7d65d0b3b7881761fa3fb2ddd166f3a23c4af7fa27.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1881b409-b1a8-5937-8228-549730fa4bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238263} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6827c7-05a6-5550-99f4-48fa5234b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238985} {:answer/id #uuid "a182d329-e4e2-53ba-9f90-9c8b26076ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239461} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d3c8be-d267-5a28-89eb-3e4892180913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239784} {:answer/id #uuid "e11de059-0ba0-587e-b3f0-03533f07de15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1881b409-b1a8-5937-8228-549730fa4bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238263} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd248ef-8f30-5d69-8f77-96405a52c5ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fefd43d0-bf25-5b03-a050-63e64e251f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104795" :question/problem "![](images/61/61c9cb5fed816e028cf47e3d6b264a40a2fc4c04bc5d330e8662a8dfd8fa26e1.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=4$ and $g(2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1d2384-537e-5cdc-89d2-9514f7f915b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478578} {:answer/id #uuid "4213e743-fdee-55e2-9a6f-9b709160ce8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478650} {:answer/id #uuid "68a6cf61-7ae6-5cd9-b610-75dedb0da673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478714} {:answer/id #uuid "74631ce5-9bb8-5134-9259-1cb8106e5159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478730} {:answer/id #uuid "b27fd78c-cdb2-5780-a4d0-dca6453eaa9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4213e743-fdee-55e2-9a6f-9b709160ce8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478650} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ccf1cc-db7d-5bc8-961d-8e2cd5b455cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "791eebb0-2b19-5413-9776-2b792618848a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104802" :question/problem "![](images/01/0115d90518526da279492096da66497c3ea1e98cbff14e66657bb08c11ea479b.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product and addition rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=2$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6765cc-049e-5b62-9580-b672a32cda03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478719} {:answer/id #uuid "6d792807-2f18-5197-8f24-fdd82e22c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478722} {:answer/id #uuid "8c25350b-5309-5136-9d9d-5ee183836fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478780} {:answer/id #uuid "9351a458-1351-5a09-a5db-0cd0f40cfff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478800} {:answer/id #uuid "aeeb74e9-b8d3-50de-adde-7867dd8afc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9351a458-1351-5a09-a5db-0cd0f40cfff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478800} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc25568f-420d-586e-a6d9-a2b442ca3305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8879cd1c-dff5-51df-b6a6-3a39f9ea79bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-105500" :question/problem "![](images/62/625de64447d959dab5a8a0da610e8687603454c331bb1c257300f222ab2e7f10.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=3f(x)-4g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=3{f}^{′}(x)-4{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=3{f}^{′}(-2)-4{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d90d88b1e2cff1e08017bde2dd8d615444187f0290704e077f974ec58ee46112.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{4-2}{0-(-4)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =3{f}^{′}(-2)-4{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f0fc67a-f9cb-5f62-890b-c45edcd08aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478694} {:answer/id #uuid "66ca1a9b-32ff-5882-ba21-4c4d6716c941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478710} {:answer/id #uuid "775e5a3d-7568-59df-85ff-a3a63889bea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478735} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8d9b98-d58b-51aa-9e35-9b06cec224a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478784} {:answer/id #uuid "a586d24f-ca55-5e42-a1a3-b5af9a04ed84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f0fc67a-f9cb-5f62-890b-c45edcd08aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478694} :answer-field/id #uuid "e08e430c-ff42-53ba-a350-2d46855a44ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3156e798-219c-5353-a742-46b2b157915b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105505" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff4df5dce9f78798b864ea1880990cb91af83c7ec5fd1ec37775d885f72bc250.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=0$ and $g(1)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,5)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,2)$ equals $-3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-3$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ac32e8-9345-5f9a-a909-3a78ec95a0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478570} {:answer/id #uuid "235ce64b-ee36-51bf-bc08-a0921f2f0e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478614} {:answer/id #uuid "391400db-a0e6-5666-8d45-28c76224dd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478638} {:answer/id #uuid "74e9d0cf-4680-554b-9ad9-6265bea7246b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478732} {:answer/id #uuid "781666f5-645b-558a-bc68-1b777528b5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "781666f5-645b-558a-bc68-1b777528b5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478737} :answer-field/id #uuid "b76cdd21-51f4-50d0-8c16-65e5594a7852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abfb0f27-f94d-54ac-a8fa-eb380c163f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-15660" :question/problem "![](images/23/23d22833ae89d7ed3f1c34a07f039031d77223350f0b23d17c6843f67f05668c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-5g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-5{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=2{f}^{′}(2)-5{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de80fcae8f07412e788df9ef202b15f5cdcf9fe8ca9a994abd8b1acd789359f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{3-1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =2{f}^{′}(2)-5{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-5\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cbbfc29-c1f0-5cc0-a8cf-211a3769caf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094760} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a58192-3909-55af-866c-3ed915fc5db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094761} {:answer/id #uuid "d4396829-44ad-5ca6-89cb-3ce661bd35ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094762} {:answer/id #uuid "eef1db51-db6a-5a47-a551-b15745b2d0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094763} {:answer/id #uuid "26a251bc-507d-59c2-9bd3-024456b8186f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a251bc-507d-59c2-9bd3-024456b8186f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094764} :answer-field/id #uuid "867d1b6b-8aa0-5380-8e4a-aee322fabc53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d64c4f89-ca1d-5e63-91b5-049fdf9385e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15663" :question/problem "![](images/28/28ab65439d4ee144f9928e3b5048045858d4207794f886622b0961893d98615a.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(2)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $g(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba431e1-82a0-51a9-9715-d31b176d6270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba431e1-82a0-51a9-9715-d31b176d6270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478863} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b927339-3d09-5637-a932-57946e40e153" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5467eedc-6dd1-5de2-a836-5a3460561247" :question/math-academy-id "q-314250" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6b4c982c1bcc4952ab74979e7d226b6c61faa2d79fbdbef9673e263acd0ff80.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9b2594-c187-5be7-9d07-24b1f6001a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a9b2594-c187-5be7-9d07-24b1f6001a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478576} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd9e075-b076-5eb4-a653-b67455fbea09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cb27567-8cd1-5a11-9aa3-6be8d252ac76" :question/math-academy-id "q-344888" :question/problem "![](images/15/156dee2459824e3bfefa3b9e252b0f028ccea6d72c6a650808f51daa874bb35f.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=2{f}^{′}(1)-{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/69/690bfc52663b966da350e6fa941057a7b20a2d9547d39cd95782059ee3c44aba.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-0}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =2{f}^{′}(1)-{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f47953-8cd5-534d-a6e7-d92d99687392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f47953-8cd5-534d-a6e7-d92d99687392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315887} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d76746d-f8db-594b-b595-1ffdb75fb65d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "982f2951-5071-5227-bbfa-016bc05059cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-344897" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7af6b87184781cf0f6a3132f375e7917bf4f4cd8ef8b0a4979ab949ac9d6b6c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $h(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6bbe664-db35-521b-98e3-b27af656624f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbe664-db35-521b-98e3-b27af656624f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478899} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92d92db-07ec-536e-b7c7-605d8d5eedb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3187c568-64de-59e3-811a-985d87754a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344900" :question/problem "![](images/59/5981116f8c9ad6e9dea29da381a0becbd997aa259a232dd10bd7395a78e976ad.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(-1)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(-1)={f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot h(-1)+f(-1)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=1$ and $h(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${h}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(-1) & ={f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot h(-1)+f(-1)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fe0189-99ac-5637-9392-a83123123131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10fe0189-99ac-5637-9392-a83123123131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478581} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ce6b76-7d56-5c1e-ab35-c635323e2536" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e322fcd2-b2f5-5ca5-b94b-ad3cb0222a99" :question/math-academy-id "q-344912" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e78aafab1786a1fa7ee8e58c80c41b246179cfcce7399324731131f40e8d1490.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=-1$ and $g(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{3}{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186171ed-7f2a-5a2e-8a93-8511e4fd64c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478592} {:answer/id #uuid "9285dffc-b44c-55a4-a1af-edd3119be32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478795} {:answer/id #uuid "9a71a15a-56af-5548-8b09-448f83fdad4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478813} {:answer/id #uuid "ab012ea6-444a-582d-84a8-3a9d9d52823f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478841} {:answer/id #uuid "e851d1e4-16a1-597b-b2b2-54f552eca857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab012ea6-444a-582d-84a8-3a9d9d52823f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478841} :answer-field/id #uuid "52d50ae0-b0bb-511d-9500-9659ad88abb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba0c1033-2092-580d-8cc6-92bb02b8740a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48932" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d23f912d6cc6614de82fe27d1911b32fd0faa7e29bd61a9549797cfabebb3458.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=r(x)$ and $y=s(x)$ are given above. Find ${q}^{′}(3)$ where $q(x)=r(x)s(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)={r}^{′}(x)\\cdot s(x)+r(x)\\cdot {s}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(3)={r}^{′}(3)\\cdot s(3)+r(3)\\cdot {s}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs, we obtain that $r(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s(3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $r(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${r}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $s(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $1.$ So, ${s}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(3) & ={r}^{′}(3)\\cdot s(3)+r(3)\\cdot {s}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e62398c-c7f2-5a90-94bd-6534d22dd4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328169} {:answer/id #uuid "99b31e0c-b9b1-53de-af5e-060da1733789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328262} {:answer/id #uuid "a8518df8-be72-56e5-a662-44b7d46a18e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} {:answer/id #uuid "dc886477-bb24-5eb3-b05d-d4dc07641d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328859} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6183e1-ed84-51b0-b43f-2a95ee508630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8518df8-be72-56e5-a662-44b7d46a18e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef80b9f7-4df8-5e89-9334-106d6c34b0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "432a3453-fc74-5b9b-8b50-79e9585f1cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-48952" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e13e109301fca50036cbf933b2a7946f3f53397f39f5e249c0d64e1f6ae78ba2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})$ where $q(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{3}{2})=1$ and $g(\\frac{3}{2})=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7bece1-3d5b-557e-9fdd-574dd0f25251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095971} {:answer/id #uuid "da644b27-d6fe-5fec-b21b-5186fdf9a24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095972} {:answer/id #uuid "ee48073b-a5d7-5262-b9ff-6e53fd8635b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095973} {:answer/id #uuid "0b765475-bebd-51fd-94d7-a9d55fbb570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095974} {:answer/id #uuid "45086bb8-6387-5a20-8912-e92976bd8c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45086bb8-6387-5a20-8912-e92976bd8c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095975} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0ea8c81-b5b1-5de1-b59e-aa87545b7110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8af564c9-d2a2-5081-ad7b-03f735d2e565" :question/math-academy-id "q-72974" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ec9b64e62efa9435f5d00126164a01628c86ab16a49f52b478e81a83e5b7d2a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(-1),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,0].$ Notice that $x=-1$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/2c/2c01b2337e4ca3a872e9f72ef1b52f0190096203ba2f0a4896cc11a028578d66.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,0)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{0-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a80bbc1-82e6-572d-9688-c46950720242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478575} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5504f0-2a46-5211-bfe5-08d776feb20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478646} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8c1701-b6bd-56ac-b283-f0ac2f89c660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478819} {:answer/id #uuid "c50d53a8-3f22-5c84-84d0-60a9de737b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478876} {:answer/id #uuid "dedd0fd9-10a5-559b-86be-8f99f23d7504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dedd0fd9-10a5-559b-86be-8f99f23d7504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478908} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc02d83-d67d-56d4-b249-62d78411d7ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19721c58-a18e-5cc3-8bbc-4270107e3b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-72989" :question/problem "![](images/13/13822734ec2aa7810758b2aa3fd6ddd9972fdeb37d5aa2afaa796419f7327976.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[0,2].$ Notice that $x=1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/2d/2ddcaccc6d4b626001ea8179faabc9b298956ad5d7cce2461e6e8c3a4fb7754f.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0.5-2}{2-0}=\\frac{-1.5}{2}=-0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1.5)=-0.75.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a92d29b-30cd-5b4e-918d-d268009f3f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478599} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdad335-a4d9-52d5-9257-56606f48a256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478865} {:answer/id #uuid "c92e8a09-aa9c-59c5-b430-c8a161654dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478881} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8f8115-8411-5aa4-91c2-044ee4d22784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478887} {:answer/id #uuid "d8148f7f-b604-58fc-9353-1b7e887e5cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbdad335-a4d9-52d5-9257-56606f48a256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478865} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8640a6-4d18-583e-b1d3-5ca7383680ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc313451-9f12-5322-b234-e99e208aefa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73000" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa4b5ffe6c0abfa7ca5583fe2304d1acc2d35fa5d8949bf9526b2efd296316cd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-2).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(-2),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-3,0].$ Notice that $x=-2$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/63/63ea83d99ec5e3ba226fea092134d8289e0e88f0b0d426a99a9b69f7fe5abe75.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,0)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-(-2)}{0-(-3)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}]}
